@*
    What colour the empire's fleet is.

    Ordinarily the flag's primary colour - the header of flags/colors.txt calls that "the fallback
    ship entity tint" - and an empire that wants something else turns this to Manual and picks. The
    game keeps it on its own screen with its own checkbox too, rather than among the flag colours.

    Drawn in each colour's own shade rather than in its ship value. The ship column is not a colour
    per name - it holds only 14 distinct values across the 72, so all six browns share 255,228,136
    and all six reds share 255,57,36 - which made the picker show six identical tiles in a row and
    is plainly not what the game shows. The game draws one shared 22x22 colour grid for all four of
    its colour buttons (gridBoxType name = "colors"), so its swatches never collapse either.

    What the ship column actually is, then, is a per-family accent rather than the hull tint the
    header claims. Until that is understood it is not something to paint a picker with. See
    docs/flag-colours.md.
*@

<div class="sem-chosen-color">
    <span class="sem-dot" style="--dot:@(View.ShipSwatch ?? "transparent")"></span>

    <span class="name">@Named</span>

    <button type="button" class="sem-toggle" aria-pressed="@(Manual ? "true" : "false")"
            title="Manual uses the colour chosen here. Automatic uses the flag's primary colour."
            @onclick="Flip">
        @(Manual ? "Manual" : "Automatic")
    </button>
</div>

@if (Manual)
{
    <div class="sem-swatches">
        @*
            No "automatic" tile. Handing the slot back is what the toggle above does, and a second
            way to do it that left the toggle on Manual would be a state with nothing in it.
        *@
        @foreach (var color in Session.Data.Database.FlagColors)
        {
            <button type="button"
                    class="sem-swatch @(color.Key == Design.Flag.ShipColor ? "selected" : null)"
                    style="background:rgb(@color.Red,@color.Green,@color.Blue)"
                    title="@Localizer.Prettify(color.Key)"
                    aria-label="@Localizer.Prettify(color.Key)"
                    aria-pressed="@(color.Key == Design.Flag.ShipColor ? "true" : "false")"
                    @onclick="() => Session.Edit(d => d.Flag.ShipColor = color.Key)"></button>
        }
    </div>
}

@code {
    [CascadingParameter]
    public required DesignSession Session { get; set; }

    private EmpireDesign Design => Session.Current!;

    private EmpireView View => new(Session, Design);

    /// <summary>Whether the fleet wears a colour of its own rather than the flag's primary.</summary>
    private bool Manual => Design.Flag.UseShipColor;

    /// <summary>The colour the fleet will be, named, whichever slot it came from.</summary>
    private string Named =>
        Design.Flag.DrawnShipColor is { Length: > 0 } key ? Localizer.Prettify(key) : "None";

    /// <summary>
    /// Turns the choice on or off, leaving the chosen colour in place either way.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The game keeps it there too - an empire switched back to Automatic still has its ship colour
    /// written in slot three - and throwing it away would make the toggle destructive.
    /// </remarks>
    private void Flip() => Session.Edit(d => d.Flag.UseShipColor = !d.Flag.UseShipColor);
}
